// Target `c`: a class's field-name table collides with a method named
// `fields`.
//
//   emo run main.emo                        # prints 2
//   emo build main.emo --target c -o /tmp/x # cc: error
//
// The generated C names the field-name table `<module>__<Class>__fields`
// and a method `fields` `<module>__<Class>__fields` too, so the two
// definitions collide — and the vtable, which wants the table, silently
// picks up the method pointer:
//
//   main.c:14:26: error: redefinition of 'main__Holder__fields' as
//                 different kind of symbol
//      14 | static const char *const main__Holder__fields[] = {"items"};
//   main.c:6:11: note: previous definition is here
//      6 | emo_value main__Holder__fields(emo_value self);
//   main.c:15:89: warning: incompatible pointer types initializing
//                 'const char *const *' with an expression of type
//                 'emo_value (emo_value)'
//
// The table's name is fixed — `<module>__<Class>__fields`, whatever the
// fields are called — so the collision needs only a method named
// `fields`, which is an ordinary accessor name on a form model. Nothing
// here is unusual. The fix is on the codegen side: mangle the field
// table (`__field_names`, say) so a member can never shadow it. The
// other four targets compile this file.

class Holder {
  def init(items Array[Int64]) {
    self.items = items
  }

  def fields() Array[Int64] {
    return self.items
  }
}

println(Holder.new(items: [1, 2]).fields().length().to_string())
